WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:09.520
Speaker 1: A new report from CNN at least five&nbsp;
former Trump White House staffers are talking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.640
the House Jan. six committee. This is a report&nbsp;
from CNN's Kaitlan Collins filed yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:24.000
and it contains some very interesting reports. At&nbsp;
least five former Trump administration staffers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.680
have voluntarily spoken with the House Committee&nbsp;
investigating the Jan. six attack on the Capitol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:37.200
Now, voluntarily has a lot wrapped up in it.&nbsp;
Some are interpreting voluntarily to mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.400
these are Trump staffers who have turned who&nbsp;
are. It has weighed on their conscience what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.160
they know. And so they came forward and said,&nbsp;
I want to speak. That is not what voluntarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.480
necessarily means. Voluntarily means they weren't&nbsp;
subpoenaed. That that's all that we can say for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:03.680
sure voluntarily means. And so it may have been&nbsp;
that these individuals had no real interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.440
speaking out about what happened, but they they&nbsp;
received a request to speak to the committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.560
and their lawyers said, or it was implied or&nbsp;
it became understood that you can decline this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.040
but then you're going to get a subpoena. And&nbsp;
a lot of people don't want to get a subpoena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.120
so we can't really read too much into voluntarily.&nbsp;
OK? Those discussions come as lawyers working for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.040
the committee have also reached out to&nbsp;
a range of other Trump aides to inquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.160
whether they would be interested in speaking with&nbsp;
the committee voluntarily without the threat of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.520
subpoena. So there it is. The five former staffers&nbsp;
who have had conversations with the committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:48.080
have done so with either members or their staff.&nbsp;
Some believe they have information worth sharing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:54.720
while others are hoping to avoid being legally&nbsp;
compelled to talk to the committee. Very exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.360
what we're talking about. Congressman Jamie&nbsp;
Raskin, who's on the committee? He's a Democrat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.200
He said on CNN yesterday quote, I've got&nbsp;
good reason to believe a number of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.440
are horrified and scandalized by what took&nbsp;
place on January 6th, and they want to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.200
their legal duty and civic duty by coming&nbsp;
forward to explain exactly what happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.240
We don't know that to be true. That's Jamie Raskin&nbsp;
claiming that we're going to continue to encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.800
everybody who has relevant information to come and&nbsp;
talk. The engagement could provide insight for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.440
committee that's seeking to learn more about the&nbsp;
actions of former President Trump and supporters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.960
according to five former Trump aides. Counsel for&nbsp;
the committee has emailed or texted them directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.120
to ask whether they are interested in coming&nbsp;
to talk to the congressional investigators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:43.280
often looking for context on what happened inside&nbsp;
the West Wing before the insurrection on January&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.800
6th. Several people have voluntarily sat down with&nbsp;
the committee. Others have declined the requests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.840
or not responded. The outrage has ranged from&nbsp;
junior level staffers to more seasoned officials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.880
The outrage is the outreach is not necessarily&nbsp;
necessarily because the committee believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.840
staffers were involved in what happened, but&nbsp;
they want to glean context on what took place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:11.760
Period. So one of the real questions here,&nbsp;
and unfortunately, you know, I still believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.920
and I know it upsets many of you. I don't think&nbsp;
there's going to be real consequences for people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.560
regardless of what the answer here is. But one of&nbsp;
the questions that we continue to seek to answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.040
is to what degree was Trump involved in planning?&nbsp;
To what degree did Donald Trump know that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.800
going to get violent? To what degree did Donald&nbsp;
Trump encourage or want exactly what happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.680
happen? We have a variety of reasons to believe,&nbsp;
including the fact that Trump spoke so positively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.960
about the rioters, even as the riot was going on&nbsp;
on January 6th in that recorded video statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:49.600
that was released. We have every reason to believe&nbsp;
that he was involved and he was enjoying it until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.120
or other than the fact that, as he said, the&nbsp;
protesters, the rioters, the insurrectionists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:01.040
looked low class, which he felt was making him&nbsp;
look bad. So the broader context is momentum is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:06.880
building in terms of the committee. We don't&nbsp;
know whether people speaking to the committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.480
are really turning on Trump. But I still don't&nbsp;
think this is going to lead to the types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.600
consequences you and I would like to see. I know&nbsp;
it upsets many of you. I know there are people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.800
the audience who when I say this, they write to me&nbsp;
and say, David, you're part of the problem. Saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.920
there won't be consequences is likely to make&nbsp;
that happen. It's a self-fulfilling prophecy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.880
I reject that completely. What I say will have&nbsp;
no bearing on whether there are consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:39.120
There are going to be no real consequences for&nbsp;
the top people. So we're talking about Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.640
We're talking about Mark Meadows. We're talking&nbsp;
about members of Congress like Mo Brooks, Lauren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.200
Boebert, Marjorie Taylor Greene, Louie Gohmert,&nbsp;
etc. There may be, you know, a sort of mild public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.360
shaming of some kind. Republicans will, of course,&nbsp;
claim it's all bogus, that none of it is deserved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:03.360
that it's all a witch hunt. There will be slaps on&nbsp;
the wrist for most of the actual insurrectionists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.560
Remember this 300 to 400 of these cases that&nbsp;
are now pending. There will be some slaps on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.960
wrist. Some people will get slightly more serious&nbsp;
sentences. We've seen a couple of them handed out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.040
few years ago. Trump's political career may just&nbsp;
kind of peter out, or he'll end up president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.680
again, which is really scary. And at the end of&nbsp;
the day, this is not going to so damage Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.200
that the potential to be president again will be&nbsp;
eliminated. It may does not happen. It may deflate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.320
Trump if this gets really bad. If what we learn&nbsp;
is really damaging. It may deflate Trump. It may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:46.000
hurt Republicans in 2022, which itself functions&nbsp;
to discourage Donald Trump from running in 2024.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.240
I hope I'm wrong. I hope that there are real&nbsp;
consequences and that they are significant, et&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.320
cetera. But it is certainly not. Seeming&nbsp;
likely to me at this point in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:02.240
if you know now here's who I want to hear from. If&nbsp;
you just want to say, David, stop repeating that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:08.640
Skip it, don't e-mail me. I get those emails all&nbsp;
the time. If you want to explain to me the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:15.680
path for how it will happen and who will hold, the&nbsp;
members of Congress allegedly involved accountable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:22.560
White House staff allegedly involved accountable&nbsp;
Trump accountable. Explain to me the sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.000
of events and how it will happen. I don't see it.&nbsp;
But if you understand the path forward on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:07:49.840
I do want to hear from you. You can&nbsp;
email info at David Pakman dot com.

